Yes. After reviewing the current neuro-oncology/imaging landscape through September 2026, I would **not** build “another AI brain-tumor segmentation tool.”

The more valuable opportunity is to build a **Neuro-Oncology Operating System**: one software environment that reconstructs the patient’s tumor as a continuously updated biological and clinical state, then uses AI to connect imaging, pathology, molecular data, treatment history, neurologic status, evidence, and clinical trials.

The central object should be your **Neuro Tumor State**.

## 1. What the field is actually struggling with

The biggest software opportunity is not a lack of images or AI models. There are already strong point solutions for segmentation, volumetrics, radiation planning and metastasis tracking. Brainlab, for example, already offers AI tumor segmentation and longitudinal retreatment workflows; Cortechs/NeuroQuant offers FDA-cleared brain-tumor segmentation, volumetrics, and longitudinal metastasis tracking. ([Brainlab][1])

The unresolved problem is:

> **Nobody wants another image viewer. Clinicians need the entire disease trajectory assembled into one interpretable state.**

### Pain-point matrix

| Current pain point                              | What is broken                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | Software/AI opportunity                      |
| ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- |
| **Progression vs pseudoprogression**            | Conventional MRI can look worse because of treatment effect rather than viable tumor. Recent literature still describes this as unresolved; one 2026 review says conventional MRI is unreliable in roughly 30–40% of post-treatment GBM cases. ([PubMed][2])                                           | Multimodal **Response Adjudication Engine**  |
| **Loss of lesion identity over time**           | Brain-metastasis reports often become snapshots instead of a continuous lesion history. In a 2026 study, continuity deteriorated sharply as metastatic waves increased, with complete loss of continuity in patients with >3 waves. ([PubMed][3])                                                      | Persistent **Lesion Identity Graph**         |
| **Serial MRI comparison is laborious**          | Prior scans, current scans, RT plans and contours frequently must be manually assembled. A 2026 workflow that automatically fused prior RT contours with follow-up MRI cut review time from 7.97 to 3.95 minutes per case and increased full inter-reader agreement from 72.4% to 93.5%. ([PubMed][4]) | Automated longitudinal imaging workspace     |
| **Scanner/protocol variability**                | Different scanners and acquisition protocols can make a biologically unchanged lesion appear different. 2026 work specifically addresses longitudinal MRI harmonization for brain metastases. ([Nature][5])                                                                                            | MRI **Quality + Harmonization Engine**       |
| **Imaging, pathology and genomics are siloed**  | Glioma care increasingly requires integrated diagnosis, but MRI, pathology, molecular testing and treatment histories are often evaluated separately. ([PubMed][6])                                                                                                                                    | **Neuro Tumor State Graph**                  |
| **Molecular diagnosis is increasingly complex** | WHO CNS5 relies heavily on molecular findings; many CNS tumor diagnoses cannot be reliably made according to current criteria without molecular testing. ([PubMed][7])                                                                                                                                 | Molecular completeness/reconciliation engine |
| **Tumor heterogeneity isn't represented well**  | A single diagnosis or biomarker can conceal different tumor cell states and spatial ecosystems. A 2026 Nature Neuroscience study integrated spatial and single-cell data from 100 GBM patients and identified reproducible malignant cellular communities. ([Nature][8])                               | Spatial/radiographic tumor habitat model     |
| **Clinical trial matching is manual**           | Eligibility depends on diagnosis, biomarkers, prior therapies, timing and other constraints. A 2026 prospective oncology study tested a knowledge-graph/neuro-symbolic approach specifically because trial screening remains complex and labor-intensive. ([PubMed][9])                                | Neuro-oncology trial intelligence            |
| **Tumor boards reconstruct the case manually**  | Radiology, neurosurgery, pathology, radiation oncology and medical oncology each hold pieces of the story. EANO explicitly emphasizes multidisciplinary coordination across these disciplines. ([EANO][10])                                                                                            | AI-generated tumor-board case state          |
| **AI itself has a deployment problem**          | Performance can shift across scanners, institutions and populations; regulatory expectations increasingly emphasize lifecycle monitoring. ([U.S. Food and Drug Administration][11])                                                                                                                    | Built-in AI governance/drift monitoring      |

