# 7. Module #5 — Molecular + Pathology Intelligence

The platform should have a **Molecular Passport**.

For example:

```text
WHO CNS STATE

Adult-type diffuse glioma
IDH: mutant
1p/19q: intact
ATRX: loss
TP53: altered
MGMT: methylated
CDKN2A/B: intact
Methylation class: ...
```

But the important part is not merely displaying the values.

The software should detect:

### Missing diagnostics

> MGMT unavailable

> Methylation classification recommended/not completed

> IDH result conflicts with pathology interpretation

> Molecular result pending

> Tissue insufficient

That directly addresses a real problem: molecular testing is now central to CNS tumor classification, yet testing practices vary substantially between institutions. ([PubMed][7])

AI can become an assistant here too.

A 2026 Nature Cancer study, Hetairos, used H&E histology to predict 102 CNS methylation-based subtypes. In a prospective routine-diagnostic evaluation reported in that study, the system reduced a molecular-testing turnaround process from 12 days to about 12 minutes for the AI-supported prediction step. That is promising evidence for workflow acceleration, but it is not a reason to replace confirmatory molecular testing. ([Nature][14])

Your system should therefore treat AI pathology as:

> **diagnostic acceleration + test orchestration**

not:

> "AI replaces pathology."

